# What a camera parameter file says, and the ring radii drawn from it.
#
# Center Setup overlays the fisheye with a crosshair at the optical centre and a
# ring at every N degrees of alpha, so an operator can see whether the centre is
# right. In v2.0 the window held a Moildev instance and called getRhoFromAlpha()
# per ring.
#
# That is a lens model evaluation -- the polynomial that maps an incident angle to
# a radius on the sensor -- so it happens here, and the client receives radii in
# pixels and draws circles. The distinction is not pedantic: getRhoFromAlpha is
# the calibration itself, and a second implementation of it on the client is
# precisely the two-copies-of-a-formula problem this architecture exists to avoid.

# The camera parameter JSON the operator picked.
string params_json

# Camera name inside that JSON, when it holds several. Empty for the first.
string camera_type

# Working resolution ratio. 1.0 is the file's own calibration resolution; the
# window drops it for a faster preview on large sensors, and every value below
# scales with it -- which is the reason it is an input rather than something the
# client applies afterwards.
float64 resolution_ratio

# Override the optical centre before computing the radii. This is what the two
# spin boxes do: the operator drags the centre and the rings must move with it.
# Send has_center false to use the file's own icx/icy.
bool has_center
int32 icx
int32 icy

# Alpha values, in degrees, to return radii for. Empty means "every 10 degrees up
# to fov/2", which is what the overlay draws by default.
float64[] alphas
---
bool success

# The file's own calibration resolution, before resolution_ratio.
int32 native_width
int32 native_height

# The resolution everything below is expressed in.
int32 width
int32 height

float64 camera_fov

# The optical centre in force -- the file's, or the override.
int32 icx
int32 icy

# Radius in pixels for each requested alpha, same order. A negative entry means
# that alpha falls outside the lens model and the client should not draw a ring
# for it, rather than drawing one at a made-up radius.
float64[] rho

string message
